  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Jordanians carry their own name, their father's name, their grandfather's name and a family name, as in Omar Khaled Mahmoud Al-Zoubi; day to day it is the first name and family name, and women keep their family name after marriage. Family names often come from a clan or tribe (Obeidat, Bataineh, Tarawneh), a home town (Nabulsi, Khalili), or a trade (Haddad, smith; Najjar, carpenter). Older people are called by kunya, Abu or Umm plus the eldest son's name (Abu Omar, Umm Omar). Christian Jordanians share most family names and carry names like Issa, Elias, George and Rita alongside the common Arabic ones. Syrian, Iraqi and Egyptian residents follow the same Arabic pattern.",
